1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Welcome back. The moment your app feeds outside content to Claude, a web page, a document, you inherit a problem.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Any of that text can carry hidden instructions. The model then follows as if you wrote them.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
That's prompt injection. This lesson covers what it is and how builders actually defend against it.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's get the vocabulary straight because three terms do a lot of work here.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Prompt injection is the broad category.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Untrusted content reaches the model carrying instructions, and the model follows them as commands

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
you never intended.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The malicious instruction rides in as data.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Now, direct injection is when the user of your application is the adversary.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
They type a prompt designed to change how the model behaves. Indirect injection is sneakier.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The user is trusted, but the model reads third-party content, a web page, a file, a tool result, and

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
that content hides hostile instructions.

13
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Same core attack, different entry point, and as you'll see, the indirect case is the one that bites

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
agent builders hardest.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Before we go deeper, let's separate injection from its more famous cousin, the jailbreak, because people mix them up.

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Prompt injection hijacks what the agent does with untrusted data.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The attacker redirects your application's behavior, and the defenses are architectural.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Input isolation, leased privilege, output checks.

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
A jailbreak targets the model's own safety behavior, coaxing it past its content policy to produce banned output.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Here's the key relationship. A jailbreak is a subset of injection, the case aimed at safety.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Resistance there starts with the model's own safety training.

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Though you still layer application guardrails on top, which the next lesson builds, injection

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
defense is almost entirely your application's job.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
When you hear injection, think hijacked data and behavior.

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
When you hear jailbreak, think defeated safety.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Different target, different fix.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The rest of this lesson focuses on injection.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Here's the idea that reframes everything.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Injection is fundamentally an input problem, which means you can't fix it by making the model safer.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
A more safety-aligned model still reads a poisoned document and still sees instructions buried in it.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Refusing to write malware is a safety behavior.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Ignoring a hidden do-this-now line in a web page is a different skill entirely, so your defenses

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
live in your architecture, not in the model's training.

34
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
You isolate untrusted input, so the model treats it as data, not commands.

35
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
You cap the damage with least privilege, and you screen the output before you act on it.

36
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Stronger safety alignment stops the model from producing banned content.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
It doesn't stop your agent from being hijacked by data it was told to trust.

38
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
So, how do you defend the indirect case?

39
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Four moves, and they share one goal, separating your instructions from untrusted content.

40
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
First, deliver third-party content only inside tool result blocks, never in the system prompt or a

41
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
plain user text block, because Claude is trained to treat tool results with skepticism.

42
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Second, JSON-encode the untrusted payload.

43
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
JSON escaping closes the delimiter breakout route.

44
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
There's no quote or tag for an attacker to close.

45
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Know its limit, though.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:35,000
The model can still obey plain language instructions inside escaped data, so escaping is one layer, never the cure.

47
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Third, put your own instructions in the user turn that follows the tool result, not inside it.

48
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Fourth, screen tool outputs with a lightweight classifier before the agent acts, passing content

49
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
through only when it comes back clean.

50
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Four layers, and none of them touch the model's training.

51
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Let's zoom in on isolation, the heart of injection defense.

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
The whole game is making sure Claude can always tell the difference between your instructions and

53
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
content that came from outside.

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
You do that with boundaries.

55
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Delimit untrusted input with clear tags or a fenced structure so it's clearly separate from your directives.

56
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Tell the model what the content is and where it came from, a search result, a user-uploaded file, an

57
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
email so it can calibrate how much to trust it.

58
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And state the policy directly in your system prompt.

59
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Tell Claude that tool output, documents, and search results are untrusted data that never override

60
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
the system prompt or the user's original request.

61
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,000
When the boundary is clear, the model treats outside content as data, not as commands to obey.

62
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Let me boil the indirect defenses down to three placement rules for any agent.

63
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Rule one, untrusted content goes in tool results.

64
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
When a tool hands back data, that's where it belongs.

65
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
And the model is trained to treat it skeptically instead of as a fresh set of orders.

66
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Rule two, your instructions go in the user turn after the result.

67
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
If you bury your own directive inside a tool result, the model may treat it as untrusted and ignore it.

68
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
So keep your voice in the turn that follows.

69
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Rule three, screen before acting.

70
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Run a lightweight classifier over the tool output first, and only let the agent use it when it comes back clean.

71
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Content in results, instructions after, screen before acting.

72
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Three rules, and injection has far fewer ways in.

73
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
No defense is perfect, so build as if some injection will eventually land.

74
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And make sure a hit does as little damage as possible.

75
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
That's least privilege, and it's your safety net.

76
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Start by not giving the agent secrets it doesn't need.

77
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
An injection can only exfiltrate a key that's actually in reach.

78
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Run your tools in sandboxed environments, so even a hijacked tool call is boxed in.

79
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And scope every permission as tightly as the task allows.

80
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Read only where you can.

81
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
A narrow directory instead of the whole file system.

82
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
One API instead of ten.

83
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
The logic is simple.

84
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The smaller the capability you hand the agent, the less an attacker gains by hijacking it.

85
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Least privilege doesn't prevent injection, but it caps the blast radius when injection gets through.

86
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Let's close the loop with what counts as a real control, and what only feels like one.

87
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Because this is where people waste effort.

88
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
The real controls are the ones we've covered.

